WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.877 --> 00:00:06.256
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

00:00:15.056 --> 00:00:19.561
The largest land animal ever to tread the surface of the Earth

00:00:19.561 --> 00:00:21.396
was a sauropoda.

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:26.443
They were enormous.

00:00:30.447 --> 00:00:34.617
Some, like Dreadnoughtus, could grow to 85 feet

00:00:34.617 --> 00:00:44.919
long, while others tipped the scales at 100 tons.

00:00:44.919 --> 00:00:47.630
That's 10 times heavier than the biggest elephant.

00:00:52.427 --> 00:00:56.014
So why were the dinosaurs so much bigger

00:00:56.014 --> 00:00:57.974
than their modern counterparts?

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:06.858
There have been a lot of strange ideas about why dinosaurs

00:01:06.858 --> 00:01:08.276
could have gotten so big.

00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:10.487
One of my favorites was that gravity

00:01:10.487 --> 00:01:13.573
has changed through time and maybe the gravity of the Earth

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:16.201
was lower in the past which have meant that the animals got

00:01:16.201 --> 00:01:19.245
correspondingly larger.

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:21.498
It's an interesting idea but an idea that's

00:01:21.498 --> 00:01:23.708
very definitely nonsense, the Earth's gravity

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:27.087
has not changed that much in the last few million years.

00:01:30.715 --> 00:01:34.594
One thing we do know is that dinosaurs could grow fast.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.183
They grew from an egg the size of a grapefruit

00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:42.894
to an absolutely enormous animal.

00:01:42.894 --> 00:01:45.396
And we have some evidence that they grew very,

00:01:45.396 --> 00:01:50.235
very, very quickly throughout their early life.

00:01:50.235 --> 00:01:52.612
Growth patterns in fossilized bones

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:57.450
show some sauropods got to full size in just 20 years.

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:05.291
That's an extraordinary 1,000 fold increase.

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:07.919
So how did they do it?

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:10.004
The sauropods were to some extent

00:02:10.004 --> 00:02:13.883
pre-adapted to be able to let them get really, really big.

00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:15.510
They had a metabolism that was probably

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:17.595
quite like that of birds which allows

00:02:17.595 --> 00:02:20.348
them to take oxygen on the in breath

00:02:20.348 --> 00:02:23.017
but also on the out breath.

00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:25.854
The evidence for that is all of the holes

00:02:25.854 --> 00:02:28.565
that we see in the vertebrae and also in their limb bones.

00:02:32.277 --> 00:02:37.782
These holes contained air sacs crucial for extracting oxygen

00:02:37.782 --> 00:02:42.662
to power their super efficient metabolism.

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:44.539
But the holes had another role.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:53.590
The biggest land animal today, the elephant, has solid bones.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:57.886
Its skeleton has reached the limit of what it can support.

00:02:57.886 --> 00:03:01.181
Much bigger and it would collapse under its own weight.

00:03:04.267 --> 00:03:06.978
But the holes in the sauropod bones

00:03:06.978 --> 00:03:10.190
meant their skeletons were comparatively lighter

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:10.982
and stronger.

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:17.572
Their posture helped too.

00:03:21.326 --> 00:03:23.745
Their legs are tucked very firmly under their bodies

00:03:23.745 --> 00:03:24.913
acting as pillars.

00:03:24.913 --> 00:03:26.831
And those pillars very efficiently

00:03:26.831 --> 00:03:28.917
allow the holding up of weight against gravity.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:34.214
These sets of features together meant

00:03:34.214 --> 00:03:36.132
that dinosaurs had a unique opportunity

00:03:36.132 --> 00:03:37.592
to reach these huge body sizes.

00:03:43.348 --> 00:03:49.062
As these plant-eating sauropods got bigger and bigger,

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:52.065
it had an impact on the evolution of the hunters living

00:03:52.065 --> 00:03:54.442
alongside them.

00:03:54.442 --> 00:03:57.070
One of the reasons why gigantic carnivores may have reached

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:58.863
their large sizes is actually simply

00:03:58.863 --> 00:04:00.865
because they're trying to keep up.

00:04:00.865 --> 00:04:03.159
They're trying in an evolutionary arms race

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:06.246
to stay in the game, to be able to take on some

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:09.707
of these very large herbivores.

00:04:09.707 --> 00:04:11.209
The predators got larger--

00:04:11.209 --> 00:04:15.171
so the sauropods got larger, so the predators got larger still.

00:04:15.171 --> 00:04:18.007
This helped the sauropods grow to these absolutely enormous

00:04:18.007 --> 00:04:20.385
sizes that we haven't seen since on Earth.

00:04:23.972 --> 00:04:26.182
It's unlikely we will ever see anything

00:04:26.182 --> 00:04:29.435
like these prehistoric giants ever again.

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:32.772
[UPBEAT MUSIC]

